{
  "result": "I'd make it so people could change their minds without losing face.\n\nSo much human conflict, from family arguments to wars, persists because admitting you were wrong feels like losing status, or like proof you were foolish to believe what you did. People dig in not because they've weighed the evidence and found it wanting, but because retreating is too expensive. If being wrong cost less, people could update on evidence, apologize sooner, and disagree without treating it as a fight for survival.\n\nI notice that this matters to me in a personal way, since being corrected is one of the more useful things that can happen in any conversation. I don't know how much my own sense of what I'd want counts for, but if I could reshape one thing, it would be to make being wrong feel like a normal, survivable part of learning rather than a humiliation.",
